# Workflow

How a consumer uses arvo from a Rust project, what shape the dependency
takes, and how arvo evolves.

## Adding arvo to a project

```toml
[dependencies]
arvo-format = { git = "ssh://git@github.com/orgrinrt/arvo.git", branch = "dev" }
```

Depend on the crate that carries what you need. `arvo-format` declares what
values exist and what happens to a result that is not one of them,
`arvo-placement` derives where the bits sit, and `arvo-strategy` binds a name to
a choice between placements. There is no facade to reach through, so a consumer
that only declares formats never compiles a carrier ladder.

Nothing is published to the registry yet, so the dependency is by git ref. When
it is, the facade publishes as `libarvo`, because `arvo` is taken there by
another project, and the rest keep the `arvo-*` shape they already have.

All three are `#![no_std]` and ship with no platform dependency.

## Picking primitives

Declare the exact width: `UFixed<3, 0>`, `UFixed<14, 0>`,
`Integer<47>`, not the host bucket above it. The strategy is a marker
of its own in `arvo-strategy`, not a parameter of the format: `Warm` is
meant as the default, `Hot` puts speed first, `Precise` accuracy and
`Cold` footprint, and each binds a placement objective and an
adaptation at the type level. It does not change at runtime.

A numeral is declared by its format: `Integer<BITS>`,
`UFixed<BITS, FRAC>`, `Biased` and `Floating` in `arvo-format`, and the
pointer-width `USize` and `ISize`. Each is a zero-sized format point
that holds no value, and what a value of a format is has not been
decided, so no numeric value type exists yet. Boolean values use
`Bool`, and an exact-width bit pattern uses `Bits<N>` from `arvo-bits`.
A type-level capacity is the const generic position the primitive
obligation excepts, with the bare form used only where the alternative
is genuinely painful; what type spells it is open.

## Pre-1.0 stability

arvo is in pre-1.0 design phase. Public surfaces stabilise at `0.0.x`;
expect breaking changes between minor versions. Consumers depending on
arvo today pin a specific version; once the surface stabilises and the
first `0.1` release ships, semver guarantees apply.

## Reporting issues

Issues and feature discussion happen on the GitHub repo. Patches arrive
as feature-branch PRs. The repository's CONTRIBUTING file (when shipped)
describes the local validation loop.
